# Regression test: fg used to just wait on a background job's receiver without ever
# resuming it first. That was harmless for a job that had simply never been paused (the
# common trailing-`&` case, where the job runs freely from the start), but for one
# paused mid-flight (e.g. via pause, or -- interactively -- Ctrl-Z) it meant the
# job just sat suspended: fg would wait on a value the job's own worker thread could
# never produce without first being resumed.
#
# No explicit timeout assertion needed: if fg doesn't resume the job, cmd's spawned
# process never exits and this script's own fg call never returns, so run_system_test's
# own timeout fails the test -- same reasoning as the sibling
# cmd_respects_job_control.crush.

$job_id := $(cmd sleep 2 &)
pause $job_id

$status := $($(jobs | where {($id == $job_id)} | select status | list:collect)[0])
assert ($status == "Paused") "job should be Paused after pause"

fg $job_id
